Let me look at the `GetSsmlAttribute` function more carefully. The vulnerability is in `GetSsmlAttribute`:

```c
static wchar_t *GetSsmlAttribute(wchar_t *pw, const char *name)
{
        int ix;
        static wchar_t empty[1] = { 0 };

        while (*pw != 0) {
                if (iswspace(pw[-1])) {
                        ix = 0;
                        while (*pw == name[ix]) {
                                pw++;
                                ix++;
                        }
                        ...
                }
                pw++;
        }
        return NULL;
}
```

The issue: `pw[-1]` is accessed when `pw` points to the first character of the string. If the string starts with a whitespace character, then `pw[-1]` reads before the start of the buffer. But more relevantly, the description says "A malformed tag like `<foo` in SSML parsing causes espeak to read past the end of the string."

Let me look at how `ProcessSsmlTag` is called and how the tag is parsed. Let me look at the caller in readclause.c.
